hash map lets us query, in O(1), which images a mask covers and what labels they receive.
Step 3: Iterative rule extraction. While uncovered images remain and at least one MSCX still covers a new image we
Step 3:1. count, for every mask S, how many new images it covers per class (NS,c);
Step 3:2. compute its total new coverage |TS| = P c |NS,c| and the associated error errS = |TS| −maxc |NS,c|;
Step 3:3. pick the mask S⋆with the smallest error, breaking ties by the largest gain gainS = |TS|;
Step 3:4. append the rule (S⋆; c⋆) = arg maxc |NS⋆,c| to C;
Step 3:5. mark all images in TS⋆as covered (U ←U \ TS⋆) and delete S⋆ from the mask dictionary so it cannot be reused.
This greedy choice minimises newly induced errors at each round while maximising the number of newly explained images.
Step 4: Default rule. Once no mask can cover a fresh image or U = ∅, we add a terminal clause whose conclusion is the majority class in D. Any image that slipped through the mask rules is now assigned a class, guaranteeing completeness.

