The main contributions of this work are hence the following:

• SD-RAG, a flexible mechanism for selective disclosure in retrieval-augmented generation pipelines that is resilient to prompt injection attacks;
• a dedicated graph-based data model to represent the data corpus and the security/privacy constraints;
• a novel mechanism to allow the ingestion of dynamic human-readable security/privacy constraints;
• an enhanced retrieval strategy to extract relevant content along with associated security and privacy constraints;
• a generative AI–based redaction module that enforces the required constraints on the retrieved

content.

In addition to the above contribution, our work also proposes a suitable strategy to evaluate SD-RAG and compare its performance with existing approaches. Accordingly, this paper makes the following additional contributions:

• an automated methodology for constructing test datasets tailored for redaction-aware closedquestion answering (RCQA);
• novel evaluation metrics for assessing the quality of redacted content, capturing both privacy preservation and the retention of safe, useful information.

